---
import Layout from '../layouts/JpegXlLayout.astro'
import manifest from '../../../capabilities/manifest.json'
const jpegxl = manifest.codecs.find((c) => c.id === 'jpegxl')
if (!jpegxl) throw new Error('Missing JPEG XL capability')
const cards = [
  [
    'convert',
    'Convert and inspect',
    'Encode lossless or static lossy JXL. Decode, resize and export.',
    'jpegxl-pixel-lossless.png',
  ],
  [
    'jpeg-recompression',
    'Exact JPEG round trip',
    'Compare size and verify the reconstructed original bytes.',
    'jpegxl-progressive-yuv420.jpg',
  ],
  [
    'animation',
    'Play and create animations',
    'Inspect frame timing or build a small sequence.',
    'jpegxl-pixel-lossless.png',
  ],
  [
    'progressive',
    'Progressive and selective access',
    'Watch real decoding stages and inspect source reads.',
    'jpegxl-progressive-yuv420.jpg',
  ],
  [
    'native',
    'Precision, color and channels',
    'Inspect numeric samples and separate native data from display.',
    'jpegxl-pixel-lossless.png',
  ],
  [
    'comparison',
    'Measured alternatives',
    'Explore quality, timing, startup and API tradeoffs.',
    'jpegxl-progressive-yuv420.jpg',
  ],
]
---
<Layout title="JPEG XL encoder and decoder" description="First-party JavaScript tools for lossless and static lossy encoding, viewing, animation, native samples and exact JPEG reconstruction.">
<section class="jxl-card quick"><h2>Quick answer</h2><p>Open a local file or try a generated sample. PureJsImage runs the codecs locally, even when your browser has no native JPEG XL support.</p><p><a class="jxl-action" href="/jpeg-xl/convert/?sample=png">Try a sample</a> <a class="jxl-action" href="/jpeg-xl/convert/#jxl-file">Open a file</a></p></section>
<section class="jxl-tool-cards" aria-label="Interactive workbench tools">{cards.map(([path,title,description]) => <article class="jxl-card"><img src={`/assets/jpegxl-tools/${path}.webp`} alt={`Actual ${title} output from the browser tool`} loading="lazy"/><h2><a href={`/jpeg-xl/${path}/`}>{title}</a></h2><p>{description}</p></article>)}</section>
<section id="jxl-workbench-heading" class="jxl-card copy"><h2>Use the browser APIs</h2><p><a href="/jpeg-xl/convert/">Pixel conversion workbench</a> · <a href="/jpeg-xl/jpeg-recompression/">Transcode eligible JPEG coefficients</a> · <a href="https://github.com/a-r-d/PureJsImage/blob/main/docs/jpeg-xl.md">API and precision guide</a></p></section>
<section id="jxl-progressive-title" class="jxl-card copy"><h2>Progressive and Range explorer</h2><p>The explorer has <a href="/jpeg-xl/progressive/">its own page</a>, including a local progressive sample.</p></section>
<details class="jxl-card copy"><summary>Current limits and qualified scope</summary><p>{jpegxl.boundary}</p><p>{jpegxl.memory}</p><p><a href="/jpeg-xl/comparison/">Dated comparison evidence and limitations</a></p></details>
<section id="faq" class="jxl-card copy"><h2>FAQ</h2><details><summary>What is JPEG XL?</summary><p>JPEG XL is an image format for lossless and lossy images. It also defines a way to carry JPEG coefficients and reconstruction data.</p></details><details><summary>Can JavaScript decode JXL?</summary><p>Yes. PureJsImage decodes its documented JPEG XL subset in first-party TypeScript in Node.js and modern browsers.</p></details><details><summary>What does pixel-lossless mean?</summary><p>Pixel-lossless output preserves the supported decoded sample values. It does not promise the same source file bytes or metadata layout.</p></details><details><summary>Can PureJsImage reconstruct the original JPEG byte for byte?</summary><p>Yes, for the documented eligible JPEG subset. Exact mode rebuilds the JPEG from parsed coefficients and reconstruction data, then compares every byte before it succeeds. The checked corpus also reconstructs each output through pinned <code>djxl</code>.</p></details><details><summary>Why can an exact JXL be larger than its source JPEG?</summary><p>Container and entropy-model overhead can still make small JPEGs grow. Select the size requirement to reject those results.</p></details><details><summary>Which JPEG files are currently eligible?</summary><p>Eligible files use the checked grayscale and three-component 8-bit Huffman baseline or progressive subset, supported marker structure, orientation absent or 1, and no ICC or the checked sRGB ICC.</p></details><details><summary>Are Exif orientation and non-sRGB ICC supported by exact transcode?</summary><p>Non-default Exif orientation and non-sRGB or malformed ICC profiles are rejected because the current JPEG XL codestream writer cannot represent those display semantics correctly.</p></details><details><summary>Does PureJsImage have a general lossy JPEG XL encoder?</summary><p>Forward VarDCT encoding is available through explicit lossy mode. The default pixel pipeline stays lossless. See the capability summary above for the qualified scope.</p></details><details><summary>Does this work in Node.js and browsers?</summary><p>Yes. The shared codec runs in both. This page performs local work in a browser worker, and Node.js uses the same portable implementation through its platform adapters.</p></details></section>

</Layout>

<script is:inline>
const legacy = location.hash;
if (legacy.startsWith('#jxl-progressive') || legacy === '#jxl-range-map') location.replace('/jpeg-xl/progressive/' + legacy);
else if (['#jxl-transcode', '#jxl-reconstruct', '#jxl-only-if-smaller', '#jxl-open-gray'].includes(legacy)) location.replace('/jpeg-xl/jpeg-recompression/' + legacy);
else if (legacy.startsWith('#jxl-')) location.replace('/jpeg-xl/convert/' + legacy);
</script>
